Advertise as DIY-2_13C-XXYYZZ instead of DCLK-XXYYZZ.

The vendor webtool's 2.13C picker scans with namePrefix 'DIY-2_13C-' and
routes by prefix to the 2_13c family, which explicitly supports the old
HMCLOCK/FF00 protocol this firmware implements (js/family_hm.js, all traffic
over 0xFF01 Long Value). A DCLK- name matches neither the picker filter nor
any routing rule, so the device is invisible to the webtool after the first
flash.

Keeping the vendor name means every booting image stays reachable: forward
updates and restore (vendor .bin back) both go through the same webtool over
the same FF00 channel. Applied to the build copy only.

Caveat: a non-booting image is still unrecoverable without SWD. The name only
rescues images that boot far enough to advertise.

--- a/src/user_peripheral.c
+++ b/src/user_peripheral.c
@@ -56,7 +56,7 @@
 static int otp_btaddr[2];                      // Bluetooth address read from OTP
 static int otp_boot;                           // Boot-related data read from OTP
 static char adv_name[20];                      // Advertised name buffer
-char *bt_id = adv_name+7;                      // Start of the Bluetooth ID within the advertised name (right after "DCLK-")
+char *bt_id = adv_name+11;                     // Start of the Bluetooth ID within the advertised name (right after "DIY-2_13C-")
 int clock_interval;                            // Clock update interval (seconds)
 int clock_fixup_value;                         // Clock drift correction value
 int clock_fixup_count;                         // Clock drift correction counter
@@ -151,10 +151,10 @@
 	ba0 &= 0x00ffffff;
 	ba0 ^= ba1;
 
-	// Build the advertised name (format: DCLK-XXYYZZ, where XXYYZZ are the last
+	// Build the advertised name (format: DIY-2_13C-XXYYZZ, where XXYYZZ are the last
 	// three bytes of the Bluetooth address)
 	u8 *ba = (u8*)&ba0;
-	sprintf(adv_name+2, "DCLK-%02x%02x%02x", ba[2], ba[1], ba[0]);
+	sprintf(adv_name+2, "DIY-2_13C-%02x%02x%02x", ba[2], ba[1], ba[0]);
 	int name_len = strlen(adv_name+2);
 
 	// If the device name hasn't been set, use the generated name
